Apply a keyboard's update at every cursor only where it means the same.

With several cursors, a keyboard that composes a word sends the whole word
and its change as one update, for the range the word occupies at the
primary cursor. The editor replaced that many characters before every
cursor with the primary's word. On an API 33 emulator with Gboard 12.4 and
WebView 149, two carets after alpha and bravo and one typed x gave alphax
and alphax, and Auto Save wrote it; a Backspace then gave alpha and alpha.
Patch 0048 stops the same overwrite for an update that changes no text. It
does not touch an update that does.

On Android, with more than one cursor, an update that reaches outside the
primary selection first meets a test for whether every cursor holds the
same text where it applies. They do when every cursor is collapsed, the
update's range lies in the cached text with the primary caret inside it
and no line break in it, and the text from as many units before each
cursor as the caret is from the range's start to as many after as the
caret is from its end is exactly the text of the range, with no two of
those spans overlapping. Replacing that many units before and after every
cursor with the update's text is then the same edit at each of them, and
the update is left to the code that was there before: the same edit at
every cursor, with the same composition bookkeeping and the same writes to
the input method's buffer. A word typed at several carets and then
autocorrected, replaced by a suggestion, capitalised, converted from kana
or romaji or redrawn by a glide, or an emoji replaced or deleted, still
reaches every cursor.

An update that fails that test goes one of three ways. Text typed at the
primary caret is typed at every cursor, as a first letter is. That is an
update whose text is the cached text of its range with the typed text
inserted at the caret, whose requested selection is collapsed right after
it, with every cursor collapsed. One character deleted before the primary
caret is deleted before every cursor, as a Backspace is: the update's text
is the cached text without it, the requested selection is collapsed where
it began, every cursor is collapsed, and no cursor would lose half of a
surrogate pair. A longer deletion is not a Backspace: Gboard 8.0.4,
recorded on a device, sends a tapped suggestion as the whole range of the
word replaced by nothing and then the suggestion typed at the caret, and
the first of those two updates, applied at every cursor, deleted the words
the other cursors hold. Anything else, a replacement at cursors that hold
different text, a suggestion, a deletion of more than one character, a
correction away from the caret, an insertion long enough to be a paste or
with a line break before its end, a cursor with a selection, applies to
the primary cursor alone. The other cursors are dropped, from source
'keyboard' with reason Explicit as a selection the input method sets drops
them, and the update then takes the path it takes with one cursor,
including the exit for an update that changes no text, so the typing that
follows meets one cursor. An update that changes no text and keeps its
selection leaves every cursor where it was, as before. One cursor, other
platforms, and an update that stays inside the primary selection are
untouched, so typing over selections made with Ctrl+D still replaces all
of them. Trimming the update to what every cursor shares was tried and is
not sound: the same change means different things at cursors that hold
different words.

Dropping the cursors writes nothing to the input method's buffer. Blink
has applied the keyboard's change to it when textupdate fires and the
model has not, so the cursor event would have put the old text and caret
back over the change and the edit that follows would have written the new
ones again, two writes that an update with one cursor never makes. The
buffer also keeps its start, which a cursor event for another reason moves
to the caret's line, away from the lines the keyboard holds. The existing
flag for a selection the keyboard set keeps the start, and a new one keeps
the writes out.

What it costs. Where the cursors hold different text, a replacement used
to overwrite the other cursors' text with the first cursor's; it is now
applied to the first cursor alone and the others are dropped. So is a
deletion of more than one character, a Backspace over an emoji (two units)
included, since one update does not say whether it is several Backspaces
or the edit of a word, and only the second is meant at cursors that hold
other words; so is a one-character deletion in which any cursor would lose
half of a surrogate pair. A one-letter word replaced through the pair of
updates above cannot be told from a Backspace, so its deletion reaches
every cursor and so does the typing that follows. A completion that only
appends to a word is typed at every cursor, as the letters would be. Where
the cursors hold the same text, what is still dropped is an update the
same-text test does not accept: one whose range does not contain the
caret, such as the correction of an earlier word with the caret at the end
of the line; one whose range holds a line break; one with a cursor that
has a selection; and one with two cursors closer than the span it
replaces. A composition that began with several cursors and lost them ends
without the end-of-composition rules, which run only when the editor can
tell what was inserted at every cursor. In the model of the undo stack
that the checks use, one Undo after the drop restores the text, and the
dropped cursors come back with it only when the typing before the drop is
in the same undo step. With two cursors on one line, an edit at the second
lies inside the keyboard's own text, so the buffer is rewritten once after
the edit, as it was before. A Backspace at the start of a line at a cursor
other than the first joins that line to the one above, as it did.

Source checks run the real emit and type methods over the real composition
and typing operations, with the model, the cursor list, the undo stack and
the EditContext replaced by adapters, on a host Node and not the Android
runtime. They cover alpha and bravo typed and deleted with two cursors,
three, two on one line and the second above the first; the same word at
every cursor, typed, deleted, replaced by a suggestion and reopened with
the caret moved; empty lines, romaji to kana and Hangul; a letter inside a
word; a correction, a suggestion, an equal update that moves the caret, a
selected second cursor, an emoji for an emoji, and deletions that would
cut a pair; two selections over equal words; and the suggestion sent as
the word deleted and then the new word typed, as recorded on a device,
with different words, the same word, three cursors, a one-letter word, a
next-word prediction and a completion that only appends. With one cursor,
and off Android, every update leaves the same records as before, buffer
writes included. So does an update whose cursors hold the same text, in 17
hand-written rows and in 14,504 random updates, in each of which the
cursors also came out with the same edit. Today's code made the same edit
at every cursor in all 85,344 small configurations of a separate run whose
spans do not overlap, and lost an edit and a cursor in the 456 whose spans
do, which is why the test asks for none. An update that drops the cursors
makes the same writes to the buffer, leaves the same caches and queues the
same restarts as the same update with one cursor, in ten hand-written
sequences, two of them with a buffer that starts above the caret's line,
and in 27,004 random ones. 55,592 of 60,000 random updates agree with a
rule written separately, and the rest are ones the adapters cannot model.
All 54,355,006 small updates of an exhaustive run agree with the rules for
typed text and for one character deleted, covering every caret, every
range, lone surrogate halves and ranges outside the text, and so do all
11,966,288 small documents of a second one with the same-text test. The
suggestion sent as two updates agrees with a rule written separately in
all 23,712 small cases of a third, where the code before this change
differs from it in at least 11,076. 22 deliberate breakages of the code,
among them either flag left out, the test that the caret lies inside the
update's range, each condition of the same-text test and the one-character
test, each fail one of those checks.

Built into the editor, on an API 29 emulator with Gboard 8.0.4 and WebView
134: two carets after alpha and bravo, one x and one Backspace, gave
alphax and bravox and then alpha and bravo in three runs, and with alpha
at both carets alphax twice and then alpha twice in two. A tap on the
suggestion alphabet with alphax and bravox left alphabet and bravox with
one caret in three runs, and with alphax at both carets alphabet on both
lines with two carets in two. On an API 33 emulator with Gboard 12.4 and
WebView 134 the same keys gave the same lines, in three runs with alpha
and bravo and in two with alpha at both carets. There a tap on the
suggestion alpha x left alpha x and a space on the first line and bravox
on the second with one caret in three runs, and alpha x and a space on
both lines with two carets in two. On a later build with this change, on
that emulator, a Backspace with the second caret on an empty line joined
that line to the one above and left one caret, and after a combining mark
at the second caret only the mark went, in two runs each. Not run on a
device: three carets, two carets on one line, and an autocorrection with
several carets. Typing after a drop was seen in two runs of Korean at two
carets on an API 36 emulator with Gboard 15.9 and WebView 134, on a build
with an earlier form of 0051: the keyboard ended the composition that was
open at the drop and composed the next keys at the cursor that stayed.

